d put more pressure with Nb5 - too sharp and theoretical for a sideline. } 5... Nf6 6. Nc3 Bc5 7. g3 O-O 8. Bg2 d6 9. O-O Be6 10. b3 a6 11. Be3) (4... Nxd4 { Black captures before we get the chance to go Nc2. } 5. Qxd4 Nf6 6. g4 d6 (6... h6 7. Bg2 d6 8. h3 g6 9. Be3 Bg7 10. O-O O-O 11. Qd2 Kh7 12. Nc3) 7. g5 Nd7 8. Bg2 e5 9. Qd1 Be7 10. h4 O-O 11. Nc3 Nc5 12. Be3 Be6 { [%cal Ge3c5,Gd6c5] } 13. Bxc5 dxc5 14. Bd5 { [%cal Ge6d5,Gc3d5] }) (4... e6 5. g3 Nf6 (5... Bc5 6. Nb3 Bb6 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. c5 Bc7 9. Nb5 O-O 10. Nxc7 Qxc7 11. Bg2 b6 12. O-O) 6. Bg2 Bc5 7. Nb3 Bb4+ 8. Bd2 Bxd2+ 9. Qxd2 O-O 10. O-O d5 11. cxd5 Nxd5 12. Nc3 Ndb4 13. Qxd8 Rxd8 14. Rfd1) 5. Nc2 Bg7 6. e4 d6 7. Be2 { We wait with Nc3 until black blocks with Nf6 - to avoid extra theory. } { [%cal Gg8f6,Gb1c3] } 7... Nf6 (7... Be6 { [Rare 7%]
Black is being tricky with the move order - we shouldn't play Nc3 on autopilot. } { [%cal Rb1c3,Rg7c3] } 8. O-O Rc8 { Black insists on not developing their knight - we continue to avoid Nc3. } 9. Nd2 { We'd like to play Nc3 but we can't wait forever for black to play Nf6.
Therefore we switch plan into b3 -> Bb2. } { [%cal Rb1c3,Gb2b3,Gc1b2] } 9... Nf6 10. b3 O-O { Nxe4 was a possible blunder: (10... Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Bxa1 12. Nxa1 forgetting that the knight moves backward) } 11. Bb2 a6 12. h3 { If black tries to prepare b5 with Qa5 we have a4.
Other useful moves in the future: Rb1, Ne3, Re1. } { [%cal Gd8a5,Ga2a4,Ba1b1,Bf1e1,Bc2e3] }) 8. Nc3 { And we transpose into the maroczy. } { [%cal Ge1g1,Gb2b3,Bc1e3,Bc1d2] } *
